From 2fbe557d93937bf4d5aa187b64d944506baa3b41 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Red Daka <emmanuel.rayan.daka@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 18:58:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 1/2] Fix broken single-item routes for stories, diaries, and
 dear-today
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Root cause: TanStack Router's flat file-based routing treats a dot in a
filename as implicit parent-child nesting. stories.$slug.tsx,
diaries.$slug.tsx, and collection.dear-today.$slug.tsx were therefore
being parented under their respective LIST routes (stories.tsx,
diaries.tsx, collection.dear-today.tsx) purely by filename accident, not
by design.

Because the list routes declare `validateSearch: validatePageSearch`
(pagination), the single-item routes inherited that same search schema.
Visiting any permalink (e.g. /stories/my-slug) triggered an unwanted
307 redirect to append `?page=1`, and — verified by booting the dev
server and inspecting the raw SSR output — the page that actually
rendered was the wrong one: the ancestor LIST route's component, not the
single-item page or its not-found state. For the three-level-deep
/collection/dear-today/$slug case this was worse: it fell all the way
back to rendering the top-level /collection route.

Fix: rename the three route files using TanStack Router's trailing-
underscore convention (stories_.$slug.tsx, diaries_.$slug.tsx,
collection_.dear-today_.$slug.tsx), which keeps the URL paths identical
(/stories/$slug, /diaries/$slug, /collection/dear-today/$slug) but makes
each route a sibling of its list page instead of a child, so it no
longer inherits pagination search validation.

Verified after the fix, via a live dev server and raw HTTP requests:
- No more spurious ?page=1 redirects on any of the three routes
- Each route now renders its own component (confirmed via SSR output
  data-tsd-source markers), not an ancestor's
- `tsc --noEmit`, `eslint`, and `vite build` all still pass clean
